ection\":\"What to Annotate 1: Specific Disease; Guideline 5: minimum necessary span\"},{\"start\":153,\"end\":187,\"text\":\"familial breast and ovarian cancer\",\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"A hereditary disease name used here as a modifier of the noun 'gene' (familial breast and ovarian cancer gene), so it is annotated as a Modifier rather than a standalone disease mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 3: Modifiers for other concepts\"},{\"start\":215,\"end\":256,\"text\":\"non-hereditary ( sporadic ) breast cancer\",\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"Refers to one specific disease: breast cancer in its non-hereditary (sporadic) form. The qualifiers express the most specific form and are kept in the contiguous span.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Specific Disease; Guideline 5: minimum necessary span\"},{\"start\":438,\"end\":455,\"text\":\"carcinoma in situ\",\"entity\":\"DiseaseClass\",\"rationale\":\"Carcinoma in situ is a stage category that covers a family of multiple specific in situ carcinomas, so it is a Disease Class rather than one specific disease.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Disease Class\"},{\"start\":459,\"end\":474,\"text\":\"invasive cancer\",\"entity\":\"DiseaseClass\",\"rationale\":\"Invasive cancer is a general category that covers a family of multiple specific invasive cancers. 'Cancer' is retained as a term, and the qualifier makes it a class-level mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Disease Class; What NOT to Annotate 4: general terms (cancer retained)\"},{\"start\":816,\"end\":829,\"text\":\"breast cancer\",\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"Names the specific disease breast cancer, the disease in which BRCA1 function is compromised. 'Cancer' is retained as a disease term.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Specific Disease\"}]}", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details"